using your pet is something that takes practice.  You have to know when and
where to move.  An interesting idea to practice would be to play a pacifist
character, that is, one who never kills anything themselves.  If you decide
that you aren't going to do any fighting, then you start to develop a sense
of when you should be moving forward and when you should retreat.  Take this
newfound experience into a wizard game, and try to let your pet do most of
the work, maybe getting a few kills here and there to keep your XP low and
get your pet's HP high



>
> Make as many pets... I do not have only one???

There are a couple of ways to get more pets.  There is a scroll that could
tame nearby monsters, there's also a spell that does the same thing.
There's also a tool (a magical harp I believe) that can tame nearby enemies
as well.  In the early game, none of these options are common, so you can't
really count on them as a strategy.

However, you can just about always count on the creation of enemy cats and
dogs, both of which can be tamed easily by throwing certain food items at
them (use t, then * to see what the letter of the food you want to throw is
in your inventory)  If you throw something they like, then they will become
a pet.  This will also work on horses, but keep in mind that horses are
herbivores and cats and dogs are carnivores.  If you have a means of keeping
them close by (magic whistle is best) then a small horde of pets can be a
great help in the early->midgame transition.


